{"subjectId":"python","id":"asyncio-deep","title":"asyncio in Depth","group":"Concurrency","order":60,"html":"<p class=\"lead\"><code>asyncio</code> runs thousands of I/O tasks on <strong>one thread</strong> by switching between them at every <code>await</code>. It's cooperative multitasking: fast and lock-free, as long as nobody blocks the loop.</p>\n<div class=\"callout callout--def\"><p><strong>Event loop</strong> — a scheduler that runs ready tasks one at a time. A task runs until it <code>await</code>s something that isn't ready (a socket, a timer); the loop then parks it and runs another task, resuming it when its I/O completes.</p></div>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-components\"></i>Coroutines, tasks &amp; futures</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Object</th><th>What it is</th><th>Created by</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Coroutine</td><td>The paused computation returned by calling an <code>async def</code> function. Does <strong>nothing</strong> until awaited or wrapped in a task.</td><td><code>coro = fetch()</code></td></tr><tr><td>Task</td><td>A coroutine scheduled on the loop — it runs concurrently with the code that created it.</td><td><code>asyncio.create_task(coro)</code>, <code>TaskGroup.create_task</code></td></tr><tr><td>Future</td><td>A low-level placeholder for a result; tasks are futures. Rarely created by hand.</td><td><code>loop.create_future()</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import asyncio\n\nasync def greet(name):\n    await asyncio.sleep(0)                # yield control to the loop once\n    return f\"hi {name}\"\n\ncoro = greet(\"asha\")\nprint(type(coro).__name__)                # coroutine  ← nothing has run yet\nprint(asyncio.run(coro))                  # hi asha    ← run() starts a loop, runs it, closes it</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrows-split\"></i>Sequential awaits vs concurrent tasks</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import asyncio\n\nasync def job(name, delay, log):\n    log.append(f\"start {name}\")\n    await asyncio.sleep(delay)\n    log.append(f\"end {name}\")\n\nasync def sequential():\n    log = []\n    await job(\"a\", 0.02, log)             # waits for a to finish…\n    await job(\"b\", 0.01, log)             # …before b even starts\n    return log\n\nasync def concurrent():\n    log = []\n    t1 = asyncio.create_task(job(\"a\", 0.02, log))   # both scheduled now\n    t2 = asyncio.create_task(job(\"b\", 0.01, log))\n    await t1\n    await t2\n    return log\n\nprint(asyncio.run(sequential()))   # ['start a', 'end a', 'start b', 'end b']\nprint(asyncio.run(concurrent()))   # ['start a', 'start b', 'end b', 'end a']</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p><code>await coro()</code> one after another is <strong>sequential</strong>. Concurrency only happens when several tasks exist at once — via <code>create_task</code>, <code>gather</code> or a <code>TaskGroup</code>.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-stack\"></i>gather</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import asyncio\n\nasync def fetch(i):\n    await asyncio.sleep(0.01 * (3 - i))   # later items finish first…\n    if i == 1:\n        raise ValueError(f\"item {i} failed\")\n    return i * 10\n\nasync def main():\n    results = await asyncio.gather(*(fetch(i) for i in range(3)), return_exceptions=True)\n    print(results)                        # [0, ValueError('item 1 failed'), 20]  ← …but order = input order\n    try:\n        await asyncio.gather(fetch(0), fetch(1))\n    except ValueError as e:\n        print(\"first error:\", e)          # first error: item 1 failed\n\nasyncio.run(main())</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-sitemap\"></i>TaskGroup — structured concurrency (3.11+)</h3>\n<p><code>async with asyncio.TaskGroup()</code> waits for every task created in it. If one fails, the others are <strong>cancelled</strong> and all errors are raised together as an <code>ExceptionGroup</code> — no orphaned tasks.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import asyncio\n\nasync def worker(n):\n    await asyncio.sleep(0.01)\n    if n == 2:\n        raise KeyError(n)\n    return n\n\nasync def main():\n    async with asyncio.TaskGroup() as tg:\n        tasks = [tg.create_task(worker(n)) for n in range(2)]\n    print([t.result() for t in tasks])    # [0, 1]  ← all done when the block exits\n\n    try:\n        async with asyncio.TaskGroup() as tg:\n            tg.create_task(worker(2))\n            slow = tg.create_task(asyncio.sleep(10))\n    except* KeyError as eg:\n        print(type(eg).__name__, len(eg.exceptions))   # ExceptionGroup 1\n    print(slow.cancelled())               # True  ← the sibling was cancelled\n\nasyncio.run(main())</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>Prefer <code>TaskGroup</code> over bare <code>create_task</code> + <code>gather</code> in new code. A bare task that nobody keeps a reference to can even be garbage-collected mid-flight — keep references or use a group.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-clock-x\"></i>Timeouts &amp; cancellation</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import asyncio\n\nasync def main():\n    try:\n        async with asyncio.timeout(0.01):          # 3.11+\n            await asyncio.sleep(1)\n    except TimeoutError:\n        print(\"timeout block\")                     # timeout block\n\n    try:\n        await asyncio.wait_for(asyncio.sleep(1), timeout=0.01)\n    except TimeoutError:\n        print(\"wait_for\")                          # wait_for\n\nasyncio.run(main())</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import asyncio\n\nasync def worker(log):\n    try:\n        await asyncio.sleep(1)\n    except asyncio.CancelledError:\n        log.append(\"cleaning up\")\n        raise                             # always re-raise CancelledError\n\nasync def main():\n    log = []\n    t = asyncio.create_task(worker(log))\n    await asyncio.sleep(0)                # let it start\n    t.cancel()                            # throws CancelledError into it at its await\n    try:\n        await t\n    except asyncio.CancelledError:\n        log.append(\"cancelled\")\n    print(log, t.cancelled())             # ['cleaning up', 'cancelled'] True\n\nasyncio.run(main())</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>Swallowing <code>CancelledError</code> (<code>except Exception</code> doesn't catch it, but a bare <code>except:</code> or <code>except BaseException</code> does) breaks timeouts and <code>TaskGroup</code> shutdown. Clean up, then re-raise.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-stack-push\"></i>Queues &amp; semaphores</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import asyncio\n\nasync def consumer(q, out):\n    while True:\n        item = await q.get()\n        out.append(item * item)\n        q.task_done()\n\nasync def main():\n    q = asyncio.Queue(maxsize=2)          # put() waits when full\n    out = []\n    workers = [asyncio.create_task(consumer(q, out)) for _ in range(2)]\n    for i in range(5):                    # the producer\n        await q.put(i)\n    await q.join()                        # every item processed\n    for w in workers:\n        w.cancel()\n    await asyncio.gather(*workers, return_exceptions=True)\n    print(sorted(out))                    # [0, 1, 4, 9, 16]\n\nasyncio.run(main())</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import asyncio\n\nasync def main():\n    sem = asyncio.Semaphore(3)            # at most 3 \"requests\" in flight\n    active = peak = 0\n\n    async def fetch(i):\n        nonlocal active, peak\n        async with sem:\n            active += 1\n            peak = max(peak, active)\n            await asyncio.sleep(0.01)\n            active -= 1\n            return i\n\n    results = await asyncio.gather(*(fetch(i) for i in range(10)))\n    print(results == list(range(10)), peak)   # True 3\n\nasyncio.run(main())</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>No lock is needed around <code>active += 1</code> here: with one thread, a task can only be interrupted at an <code>await</code>.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrow-bar-to-right\"></i>Blocking code: <code>asyncio.to_thread</code></h3>\n<p>A blocking call (<code>time.sleep</code>, <code>requests.get</code>, heavy CPU) freezes the <strong>whole loop</strong> — every other task stops. Push it to a worker thread:</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import asyncio, time\n\ndef blocking_io(x):\n    time.sleep(0.01)                      # a blocking library call\n    return x * 2\n\nasync def main():\n    results = await asyncio.gather(\n        asyncio.to_thread(blocking_io, 1),   # runs in the default thread pool\n        asyncio.to_thread(blocking_io, 2),\n    )\n    print(results)                        # [2, 4]\n\nasyncio.run(main())</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-repeat\"></i>Async iterators &amp; context managers</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import asyncio\nfrom contextlib import asynccontextmanager\n\nasync def ticker(n):                      # async generator\n    for i in range(n):\n        await asyncio.sleep(0)\n        yield i\n\nlog = []\n\n@asynccontextmanager\nasync def connection(name):               # async with → __aenter__ / __aexit__\n    log.append(f\"open {name}\")\n    try:\n        yield name.upper()\n    finally:\n        log.append(f\"close {name}\")\n\nasync def main():\n    squares = [i * i async for i in ticker(4)]   # async comprehension\n    print(squares)                        # [0, 1, 4, 9]\n    async with connection(\"db\") as conn:\n        log.append(f\"using {conn}\")\n    print(log)                            # ['open db', 'using DB', 'close db']\n\nasyncio.run(main())</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-alert-triangle\"></i>Common mistakes</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import asyncio\n\nasync def save():\n    return \"saved\"\n\nasync def main():\n    result = save()                       # forgot await → just a coroutine object\n    print(type(result).__name__)          # coroutine\n    result.close()                        # (silences \"coroutine was never awaited\")\n    print(await save())                   # saved\n\nasyncio.run(main())</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Mistake</th><th>Symptom</th><th>Fix</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Forgetting <code>await</code></td><td><code>RuntimeWarning: coroutine … was never awaited</code>; nothing happens</td><td><code>await</code> it or create a task</td></tr><tr><td><code>time.sleep()</code> / <code>requests</code> in a coroutine</td><td>All tasks freeze</td><td><code>await asyncio.sleep()</code>, async libraries, or <code>asyncio.to_thread</code></td></tr><tr><td>CPU-heavy loop in a coroutine</td><td>Latency spikes for every other task</td><td><code>loop.run_in_executor(ProcessPoolExecutor(), …)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Calling <code>asyncio.run</code> inside a running loop</td><td><code>RuntimeError</code></td><td><code>await</code> the coroutine instead</td></tr><tr><td>Fire-and-forget tasks without a reference</td><td>Task silently disappears; errors never seen</td><td>Keep references / use <code>TaskGroup</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrows-exchange\"></i>Sync vs async at a glance</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Sync</th><th>Async</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>def f():</code></td><td><code>async def f():</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>f()</code></td><td><code>await f()</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>time.sleep(1)</code></td><td><code>await asyncio.sleep(1)</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>for x in it:</code></td><td><code>async for x in ait:</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>with cm:</code></td><td><code>async with cm:</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>threading.Lock</code></td><td><code>asyncio.Lock</code> (only across <code>await</code>s)</td></tr><tr><td><code>queue.Queue</code></td><td><code>asyncio.Queue</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>ThreadPoolExecutor.map</code></td><td><code>asyncio.gather</code> / <code>TaskGroup</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>How is asyncio different from threading?</summary><div><p>asyncio uses one thread and <strong>cooperative</strong> switching: tasks yield only at <code>await</code>, so there are no surprise interleavings and tasks are very cheap (thousands are fine). Threads are <strong>pre-emptive</strong>: the OS can switch at any bytecode, so shared data needs locks, and each thread costs more memory. Neither gives CPU parallelism under the GIL.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What happens if you call a blocking function inside a coroutine?</summary><div><p>It blocks the event loop thread, so every other task stalls until it returns. Use the async equivalent, or offload with <code>await asyncio.to_thread(func, …)</code> (or <code>run_in_executor</code> with a process pool for CPU work).</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>gather vs TaskGroup?</summary><div><p>Both run awaitables concurrently. <code>gather</code> returns results in order and, by default, propagates the first exception while leaving the other tasks running (or collects errors with <code>return_exceptions=True</code>). <code>TaskGroup</code> (3.11+) guarantees structure: if any task fails, the rest are cancelled and all failures are raised as an <code>ExceptionGroup</code>.</p></div></details></section>"}